//! Product requirements, feature contracts and architecture decisions.

#[doc = "# Mealshop\n\nRecipes, informed nutrition, shopping and family meal planning. **Early development:** a\nRust HTTP server includes a curated ingredient catalogue (F02/F23, accepted in PRs #4/#5),\nplus a local recipe authoring/persistence slice (F04/F18, review pending).\nThe broader product features below remain planned.\n\n[![CI](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/actions/workflows/ci.yml)\n[![Audit](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/actions/workflows/audit.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/actions/workflows/audit.yml)\n[Documentation](https://th3link.github.io/mealshop/) · [Feature specs](../page_specs_readme_md/index.html) ·\n[Requirements](../page_docs_requirements_md/index.html) · [ADRs](../page_docs_adr_index_md/index.html)\n\n## Vision\n- Ingredient database, precise configurable units, scalable recipes, macro- and micronutrients\n  per serving, and evidence-backed explanations of beneficial food components.\n- Shared recipes and later paid recipes/collections; source-link-only sharing for imported recipes.\n- Shopping lists with recipe provenance, extra items, preferred shops, learned aisle order and barcodes.\n- Family meal plans with attendance, intolerances, preferences, cooking time and ongoing feedback.\n- Guided cooking with preset timers, multi-day bread/pizza schedules, and later brewing/fermentation.\n- Inventory, offers, customer-operated link imports and MCP recipe tools.\n\n## Run the ingredient and nutrition demo\nInstall stable Rust (declared minimum: 1.85), then:\n```sh\ncargo run --locked\ncurl --fail http://127.0.0.1:3000/health\n```\nThe response is `ok` plus a newline. `MEALSHOP_LISTEN_ADDR` overrides `127.0.0.1:3000`.\nOpen [the demo](http://127.0.0.1:3000/) to calculate recipe/serving nutrients and inspect\nsources, unknowns and component evidence. The English-base catalogue contains 313 ingredients\nwith German names: 284 source-backed BLS/USDA profiles and 29 explicitly unknown product identities.\n[Data and licences](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/blob/main/data/README.md) · [Demo, API and custom catalogue guide](../page_docs_guides_nutrition_md/index.html).\nRecipes can be created, saved, loaded and revised using catalogue ingredients; per-serving\nestimates retain their saved source snapshots. The executable stores data in `var/recipes`\n(or `MEALSHOP_RECIPE_DIR`) and requires loopback access until authentication exists.\n[Recipe API, storage and recovery guide](../page_docs_guides_recipes_md/index.html).\nUse Ctrl-C for graceful shutdown. A saved recipe can be previewed at another serving count. Optional protected local household profiles use an operator token; see [household setup and limits](../page_docs_guides_household_md/index.html). Hosted authentication remains future work. Tests: `cargo test --locked`. Full gate: [development guide](../page_docs_guides_development_md/index.html).\n\nThe current browser surface is a pragmatic test demo, not the product UI. It supports German\nand English interface/quantity presentation; untranslated source content stays explicitly original.\nA native Vue 3 Nextcloud client now covers shared-household recipes, profiles, planning and the catalogue ([setup and verification](../page_docs_guides_nextcloud_md/index.html), review pending). The standalone Vue shell remains planned ([F22](../page_specs_0022_product_web_clients_specification_md/index.html));\nfull cross-client localization is tracked in [F21](../page_specs_0021_internationalization_specification_md/index.html).\n\n## Deployment and business model (planned)\nRust/Tokio/Axum server, standalone or as a Nextcloud `ExApp`; Flutter client connecting to an\nown server or our hosted service. Storage options: Git-backed files, PostgreSQL, SQLite and\nMariaDB. Nextcloud installations remain free. Hosted accounts have free and paid tiers.\nThe distributed app costs a few euros; self-compilation and installation remain possible.\nOnly paid, signed app releases are intended to connect to our operated service; technical\nadmission and licensing details need validation (ADR 0004). Future federation may exchange\nrecipes and compatible meal plans between independent servers.\n\n## Contribute\nStart with [AGENTS.md](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/blob/main/AGENTS.md), the [roadmap](../page_docs_roadmap_md/index.html) and an affected feature spec.\nThe repository uses spec-driven development inspired by Curvyo and CI adapted from\nhomeautomation; exact source revisions and adaptation notes are in [provenance](../page_docs_provenance_md/index.html).\n\n## License\nAGPL-3.0-only. See [LICENSE](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/blob/main/LICENSE). Paid distribution does not remove recipients' rights\nto use, modify or redistribute the software under that licence.\n\nProtected family planning links saved recipe revisions with household constraints, preparation\nbudgets and consented calendar attendance imports. Changes have an explicit shopping-quantity\npreview before apply. See the [family planning guide](../page_docs_guides_meal_planning_md/index.html).\n"]
pub mod page_readme_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0001: stack and delivery\n\nStatus: Accepted for bootstrap; future integrations planned\nDate: 2026-11-07\n\n## Context\nThe customer requests Rust/Axum, a Flutter app, Nextcloud `ExApp` deployment and the homeautomation CI baseline.\n\n## Decision\nUse a single Rust package with Tokio/Axum and a small reusable router plus executable. Declare Rust 1.85 (edition 2024 baseline) and verify it in CI. Ship only /health now. Publish embedded project docs through rustdoc and Pages. Adapt the upstream workflows to a host binary, keeping build, coverage, MSRV, SPDX SBOM, audit, licences, Renovate and size metrics.\n\n## Alternatives\nSeparate crates now would add unused boundaries. A different backend or frontend stack would contradict the request.\n\n## Consequences and open questions\nFlutter, Nextcloud `AppAPI` authentication and packaging need later integration specs. A healthy process is not proof of database readiness. Metrics are binary size, not latency or nutrition accuracy.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0001_stack_and_delivery_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0002: quantities and nutrition\n\nStatus: Proposed implementation; customer unit preferences accepted\nDate: 2026-11-07\n\n## Context\nRecipes accept varied input while the customer wants only grams/kilograms in personal views. Nutrition and educational effects require provenance.\n\n## Decision\nRetain exact decimal input and unit, dimension, source and conversion context. Normalize mass to grams, volume to millilitres and counts to pieces within their own dimensions. Convert across dimensions only with ingredient/state-specific density or piece mass. Keep serving scaling rational/decimal until display. Persist source values and conversion versions for reproducibility. Nutrition per serving includes units, reference basis, source, missingness and uncertainty. Educational component notes carry evidence and conditions.\n\n## Alternatives\nStoring everything as grams loses volume/count meaning and needs unavailable densities. Bare binary floating point and universal teaspoon conversions obscure precision. Preserve-only-without-normalization complicates aggregation.\n\n## Consequences and open questions\nNumeric library/scale, regional spoon standards, nutrient provider and cooking retention factors are unresolved. No conversion is fabricated. Display preferences never rewrite recipe source data.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0002_quantities_and_nutrition_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0003: persistence and data boundaries\n\nStatus: Customer storage targets accepted; schema and adapters proposed\nDate: 2026-11-07\n\n## Context\nThe same household data must work in Git/files, PostgreSQL, SQLite and MariaDB, standalone or in Nextcloud.\n\n## Decision\nDefine versioned domain records with stable identities, explicit provenance, household ownership and recipe revisions before an adapter. Implement adapters incrementally with shared behavioural fixtures. Keep Git history/merge semantics separate from SQL transactions. Require backup/export/import validation and conflict handling. Do not select an ORM or create a generic repository trait in the bootstrap.\n\n## Alternatives\nSQL-only would exclude Git/files. A single SQL dialect assumed across all engines is unverified. Git does not substitute for authorization or concurrent-write control.\n\n## Consequences and open questions\nFirst adapter, offline sync, encryption, attachment storage and transaction boundaries need feature-level design. Plans/purchases/stock movements must not silently overwrite one another.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0003_persistence_and_data_boundaries_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0004: licensing hosting and sharing\n\nStatus: AGPL and business goals accepted; admission/commerce/federation proposed\nDate: 2026-11-07\n\n## Context\nThe customer requests AGPL-3.0 software, free Nextcloud installations, paid official app distribution, optional self-builds, hosted tiers and later paid content/federation.\n\n## Decision\nLicense project code AGPL-3.0-only with complete source. Treat app purchase/hosted service entitlement as service policy, separate from software rights. Validate platform attestation plus server-side account entitlement before implementing official-client admission; never rely on a secret in an open client. Preserve source-link-only sharing on every output path for imported recipes. Purchased recipe content needs a separate explicit content licence and provenance. Federation is opt-in and later.\n\n## Alternatives\nObscuring source or prohibiting AGPL redistribution conflicts with the stated licence. A signature alone does not establish account payment or prevent all custom clients. Republishing imported content contradicts the customer sharing restriction.\n\n## Consequences and open questions\nPlatform feasibility, AGPL compliance of admission terms, content rights, refunds/tax and federation trust remain gates before commercial launch. No restriction on self-hosting or self-building is introduced by this bootstrap.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0004_licensing_hosting_and_sharing_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0005: privacy and learning\n\nStatus: Proposed safeguards for customer-requested learning\nDate: 2026-11-07\n\n## Context\nMeal planning combines household attendance, dietary constraints, ratings and shopping behaviour. These can reveal sensitive personal information.\n\n## Decision\nScope records and MCP tools to authorized households. Separate hard exclusions from soft preferences; unknown allergen data needs review. Store check-off/cooking/rating events with correction and deletion support. Start from explainable rules; evaluate opt-in incremental learning against that baseline. Keep manual overrides, history reset and opt-out. Calendar and external lookups disclose what leaves the server and request appropriate consent.\n\n## Alternatives\nUnconstrained collaborative training could expose health information or override exclusions. Cold-start ML alone has no useful evidence. Silent calendar writes exceed the request.\n\n## Consequences and open questions\nAuthentication, retention, model choice, external APIs and consent UX remain open. Imported text/remote MCP content is untrusted data, not executable instructions.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0005_privacy_and_learning_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0006: durable process schedules\n\nStatus: Proposed shared process model\nDate: 2026-11-07\n\n## Context\nCooking, multi-day dough and brewing need timers, dependencies, phase reminders and equipment-specific variants.\n\n## Decision\nModel a process instance as versioned steps with dependencies, active/passive durations, target windows, equipment and persisted deadlines/completion. A server scheduler dispatches idempotent reminders with acknowledgement state. Clients show delivery limits and recover deadlines on reconnect. Preview downstream effects when delays or target times change. Store absolute instants with intended local timezone for civil-time scheduling.\n\n## Alternatives\nA transient in-app countdown cannot survive restarts or multi-day schedules. A separate scheduler per food type duplicates dependency logic. Automatic equipment control is beyond the request.\n\n## Consequences and open questions\nNotification providers, offline authority, retries and daylight-saving semantics need tests. Fermentation duration and notification delivery remain uncertain; no guaranteed outcome or safety claim.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0006_durable_process_schedules_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0007: localization and web clients\n\nStatus: Customer direction accepted; product integration details proposed\nDate: 2026-11-07\n\n## Context\nAfter merging PR #4, the German-speaking customer requested internationalization and\nexplicitly classified the existing browser workbench as a pure demo. Further demo design\ninvestment should stay pragmatic; substantial UX/UI work should be handed to a dedicated\nUX/UI agent when the customer chooses to involve one. Nextcloud and the standalone server\nneed product web clients, with Vue 3 as the preferred low-friction direction. Flutter remains\nthe separate app target from the original brief.\n\n## Decision\n- Keep `src/web/` a disposable developer/test demo, visibly labelled as such. Use it to try\n  domain/API behaviour; do not develop a product design system or Nextcloud shell there.\n- Introduce F21 for cross-cutting i18n: interface messages, localized ingredient/content names,\n  language negotiation/fallback, number/date/plural presentation, exact locale-aware quantity\n  entry and stable language-independent identities/contracts. Start with German and English.\n- This change adds a small de/en demo message catalogue, record-owned ingredient\n  display names, exact decimal formatting/input and explicit original-language content fallback.\n  It does not introduce a production translation service or localize stored nutrient values.\n- Plan F22 for Vue 3 product web clients: a Nextcloud-hosted shell using compatible Nextcloud\n  components and AppAPI-aware asset/API routing, and a standalone server web shell. Share\n  domain API contracts and translation semantics, while letting host navigation/auth differ.\n- Choose concrete packages, compatibility targets and component-sharing boundaries when F22\n  becomes Ready. Do not install Vue/build tooling or start a framework migration in this slice.\n\n## Evidence and alternatives\nNextcloud's [JavaScript standards](https://docs.nextcloud.com/server/stable/developer_manual/getting_started/coding_standards/javascript.html)\nrecommend Vue and its provided components for UI consistency. The\n[ExApp overview](https://docs.nextcloud.com/server/stable/developer_manual/exapp_development/development_overview/ExAppOverview.html)\ndescribes a Vue frontend with proxy-aware loading paths. These support the requested direction;\nthey do not prove compatibility of the current root-relative demo with a Nextcloud installation.\n\nGrowing the demo into the product would mix a debugging surface with host integration and\nconsume design effort the customer explicitly does not want spent now. Starting two full web\nprojects immediately would add unrequested implementation before the UX and integration specs.\n\n## Consequences\nF02 is accepted through merged PR #4; its browser surface remains demo-only. F21/F22 retain\nseparate completion criteria. The first i18n slice leaves source metadata, uncertainty and\neducational content in the original language with an explicit label when no reviewed\ntranslation exists. Full versioned ingredient/recipe/content translations are later F21 work.\nThe demo may remain framework-free or be removed once product clients provide equivalent\nreview flows. A UX/UI agent is not spawned by this decision; the customer decides when to involve one.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0007_localization_and_web_clients_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0008: curated ingredient sources and English-base identities\n\nStatus: Customer scope accepted; import/model details proposed\nDate: 2026-11-07\n\n## Context\nThe customer requested a broad kitchen catalogue and clarified that everything coded needs\nan English base plus identifiers for language changes. German flour types and dairy grades\nmust be distinguished rather than approximated by generic US foods. BLS 4.0 provides native\nEnglish/German names and detailed component provenance; USDA complements it.\n\n## Decision\n- Canonical ingredient IDs remain stable English slugs; category IDs are English identifiers.\n  `name`, `aliases` and `form` are the English base. Optional `translations.<language>` contains\n  localized names/aliases/forms, keyed by ingredient identity and field. English remains the base,\n  never an overriding entry in the translation map. Old schema-1 files default to English/other\n  category with no translations, so existing operator fixtures remain loadable.\n- Curate exact source-record selections in a separate manifest. Commit selected source facts,\n  original descriptions, nutrient units, acquisition methods/references, source sheet/row or numeric\n  JSON tokens and archive/workbook hashes. Regenerate the runtime catalogue deterministically.\n- BLS source data retain CC-BY-4.0 attribution to Max Rubner-Institut, version 4.0 (2025), DOI\n  10.25826/Data20251217-134202-0. USDA SR Legacy data retain CC0/public-domain status. Software\n  stays AGPL-3.0-only; provider licences are not relabelled as AGPL.\n- Runtime amounts support six decimal places. Imported source representations are rounded once\n  to that precision using half-up normalization; original cell/token strings remain in snapshots.\n  Missing, negative/unrepresentable and below-resolution positive values remain unknown, not zero.\n- BLS `CHO` maps to `available_carbohydrate`; it does not populate USDA `carbohydrate` (by difference).\n  BLS `FOLFD` and USDA nutrient 1187 map to `food_folate`; total folate/DFE stay separate. Units and\n  basis are checked before importing. No energy, fibre or carbohydrate value is inferred from another.\n- Unresolved product identities exist with no nutrient profile. Do not substitute related flour,\n  a neighbouring fat grade, a brand formula or a generic whey reference for the requested variant.\n- The demo chooses language by message IDs and translation fields; API numbers remain dot-decimal.\n  Additive error/warning codes support localized presentation without matching English sentences.\n\n## Alternatives\nUsing only USDA would miss German flour types and fat grades. Importing all foods without\nreview would add composite dishes and ambiguous mappings. Assigning plausible averages would\nhide source gaps. Mixing available and by-difference carbohydrate would produce misleading totals.\n\n## Consequences\nThe curated catalogue contains 313 items; 29 intentionally remain identity-only. The current\n17-definition nutrient registry is a subset of the providers' data, not exhaustive coverage.\nThe file-based demo catalogue is close to its pre-existing 1 MiB validation bound; future wider\ncoverage belongs with explicit storage/loading work rather than an unreviewed bound increase.\nFull versioned content translations and production language negotiation remain F21 work.\n\n## Primary sources\n- [BLS 4.0 download and licence](https://blsdb.de/download).\n- [Max Rubner-Institut BLS overview](https://www.mri.bund.de/de/institute/ernaehrungsverhalten/forschungsbereiche/bundeslebensmittelschluessel-bls/).\n- [USDA downloads](https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/download-datasets/) and [API/data licence](https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/api-guide/).\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0008_curated_ingredient_sources_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0009: local recipe revisions and pinned nutrition inputs\n\nStatus: Proposed implementation for the customer-authorized recipe slice; review pending\nDate: 2026-11-07\n\n## Context\nThe customer accepted PR #5 and requested creating/saving recipes with catalogue ingredients\nand nutrition per serving. F04 needs a usable persistence slice before the broader F18 adapters,\nauthentication, Git synchronization or product clients are ready.\n\n## Decision\nStart with a dedicated local directory, one household per server, without additional crates.\nEach immutable schema-1 JSON revision includes a stable recipe ID, monotonically increasing\nrevision, author-supplied title/language/author, exact gram strings, batch serving count,\npreparation flag, ordered instructions with active/passive seconds, and the full referenced\ncatalogue subset/version. An empty instruction list is allowed for drafts. User text is\nretained in its declared en/de language; coded fields/messages remain English identifiers.\nAuthor is attribution text, not an authenticated identity. This slice supports locally authored\nrecipes only; imports and their sharing constraints remain F16.\n\nPOST creates a recipe; PUT requires the expected latest revision. GET retrieves latest or\nan explicit historical revision; listing returns latest summaries. Nutrition is calculated\nfrom the saved snapshot, retaining unknowns, provenance and definition-specific partial totals.\nA new revision deliberately uses the current server catalogue; old revisions remain unchanged.\nChanging servings repartitions the same batch; proportional ingredient scaling is deferred.\n\nUse exclusive `create_new` directory locking for the application lifetime, plus an in-process\nmutex. Filesystem operations run on blocking workers. Write a private `.pending` file, sync\nits contents, publish using a same-directory hard link (fails if a target already exists),\nsync the directory on Unix, remove `.pending`, then sync the directory again. Only acknowledge\nsuccess after publication and synchronization. An ambiguous I/O failure makes further writes\nunavailable until operator recovery; no silent overwrite or success response. Load validates\nall records and consecutive histories before accepting traffic. Linux/local filesystems with\nhard-link and fsync support are the initial deployment target; network filesystems, external\nwriters and cross-platform crash durability have not been validated.\n\nThe executable only binds loopback until authentication exists. Recipe responses use\n`Cache-Control: no-store`. The embedding library explicitly documents its trusted-client\nboundary. New directories/files use Unix 0700/0600 (existing directory permissions stay intact).\nLimits: 128 KiB request bodies, 100 ingredient lines, 100 steps, 1–1000000 integer servings,\n1000 total revisions and 2 MiB per persisted revision. The catalogue loader bound stays 1 MiB.\nThis intentionally bounded in-memory revision index must be replaced/expanded under F18.\n\n## Alternatives\nBrowser localStorage would not satisfy server persistence or reproducible source retrieval.\nStarting all SQL/Git adapters together would delay the requested authoring slice. A mutable\nsingle JSON file would obscure revision conflicts/history; Git commits alone do not provide\napplication-level transactions or household authorization. No repository trait/ORM is needed.\n\n## Consequences\nAn exclusive lock survives crashes as a stale file and needs deliberate operator recovery.\nBackups/restore, ambiguous-write recovery and startup failures are documented in the\n[recipe guide](../page_docs_guides_recipes_md/index.html). No automatic Git commit, deletion, synchronization,\nrecipe translation, authentication or production web design is included. F04/F18 remain\nIn progress until their broader contracts and customer acceptance are complete.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0009_local_recipe_revisions_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0010: protected local household profiles\n\nStatus: Proposed implementation for the customer-authorized F09 slice\n\n## Context\nF03/F04 are accepted. F09 covers household members, hard dietary constraints and soft dislikes.\nRecipe sharing/collections remains F05. Household health data needs explicit access controls\nbefore profile storage; hosted identity integration remains separate future work.\n\n## Decision\nProvide an opt-in local household operator Bearer credential, distinct from public catalogue\nand recipe routes. All profile reads/exports/writes/removals and dietary reviews require it.\nKeep one household per dedicated directory with schema/revision validation, exclusive locking,\nprivate file permissions and atomic snapshot replacement. Token stays in runtime configuration\nand page memory, never stored with profiles. Replacement snapshots allow correction/deletion\nwithout an immutable health-data history. No new crate is needed.\n\nKeep allergy/intolerance notes, hard ingredient exclusions and soft dislikes separate. Group\nreview unions hard constraints, conservatively resolves known ingredient aliases and reports\nunknowns. Without complete allergen mapping, every unblocked result requires human review;\nno safety claim or automatic interpretation of free text is made.\n\n## Alternatives\nUnauthenticated profiles would expose household data through the existing demo. Reusing the\nimmutable recipe history would retain deleted health data. Building a hosted identity system\nhere would extend beyond this local slice. One operator credential is a bounded first access\nmodel; it does not implement multiple household roles or federated authorization.\n\n## Consequences\nThe operator credential grants access to all members. Rotation requires restart; previously\nexported copies/backups cannot be revoked. Profile storage and recovery must remain separate\nfrom recipe storage. See the household operator guide. Hosted authentication and richer\nallergen data remain explicit future work; F09 is complete for the documented local access contract.\n\n## Completion amendment\nStructured hard allergen groups and positive source-backed classifications now augment free-text\nnotes; read-only credentials may export/evaluate but cannot modify/remove profiles. Unknown\ncoverage still requires review. These changes complete the local F09 acceptance contract.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0010_local_household_access_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0011: protected family meal plans and preview/apply\n\nStatus: Implementation for customer-requested F10; review pending\n\n## Context\nF03/F04/F09 are accepted. Family planning needs pinned recipe inputs, current household\nconstraints, explicit attendance and time budgets, and inspectable changes before persistence.\nNo calendar provider, external identity service or shopping-list provider has been selected.\n\n## Decision\nAdd protected planning routes to the household access boundary. Recipes and planning share one\nlocked recipe store, so plans refer to actual immutable local recipe IDs/revisions. Household\nprofiles and plans use separate revisioned snapshots in the same exclusively locked household\ndirectory. All I/O-heavy planning work runs on blocking workers with a consistent lock order:\nrecipes, profiles, plans. A preview returns actual profile/plan revisions; apply recomputes the\nsame constraints while holding those locks, requires the matching revisions and explicit review\nacknowledgement, and never permits a known hard dietary or time conflict. Readers can inspect,\npreview and request suggestions; only operators can apply/remove. No new dependency is needed.\n\nPlans identify a Gregorian date and named meal slot, pinned recipe revision, selected attendees,\ntarget servings and active/elapsed/advance budgets. Duration estimates conservatively sum\nordered step active/passive seconds (no inferred overlap). Missing steps mean timing unknown.\nAdvance lead is elapsed duration beyond the meal-day window. Times refer to the original\nrecipe yield and are not silently extrapolated for scaled batches; this uncertainty is shown\nin the mandatory preview. Nutrition retains F03 rational scaling. Shopping requirements derive\nfrom the saved plan's pinned recipes; preview computes the signed mass delta for a slot before\nreplacement/removal. This is planning-derived shopping demand, not completion of F06's shop,\nextra-item, purchase or inventory workflow.\n\nCalendar access is an explicit read-only attendance snapshot import: source, event ID, date\nand already mapped household member IDs. User consent is required; the source/proposal remains\nvisible. Manual attendance can override it without changing any external calendar. Snapshots\ncan be supplied through the API or the demo's bounded JSON file import. No live OAuth provider,\nICS recurrence/timezone parser, calendar polling or external write is claimed. Provider adapters\ncan later supply this same bounded contract; the current import is an actual local input path.\n\nSuggestions evaluate up to 50 explicitly selected candidate revisions, reject known conflicts\nwith reason codes and rank remaining candidates by soft dislikes. Unblocked suggestions remain\nreview-required under F09. Plans retain the profile revision and allergen reference version\nreviewed when applied. Later profile/reference changes mark slots for renewed review; they do\nnot rewrite historical choices or approve them automatically.\n\n## Alternatives\nApplying arbitrary client-authored recipes would bypass pinned source/history guarantees.\nAutomatic application or using soft preferences to bypass exclusions is incompatible with F09.\nA provider-specific calendar integration without a chosen provider would introduce unnecessary\ncredentials/dependencies. Mutating an external shopping list would exceed the current F06 scope.\n\n## Consequences\nOne household/operator boundary remains local and loopback-only. Plan snapshots are bounded to\n100 slots/1 MiB and use synced private pending-file publication with fail-closed recovery.\nAPI export and backups may contain attendance/calendar identifiers. Profile deletion leaves\nhistorical plan member IDs; operators can explicitly remove those meal slots. Source data and\ncooking-time estimates are not food-safety guarantees. See the meal-planning operator guide.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0011_family_meal_planning_md {}

#[doc = "# ADR 0012: native Nextcloud client and shared household boundary\n\nStatus: Accepted, 2026-11-09; verified native client and test-container integration (see F22 validation).\n\n## Context\nBefore this change, the repository served a loopback-only single-household Rust API and a disposable\nframework-free demo. Customer requests a complete native Nextcloud 35 client and chose shared\nhouseholds with explicit invitations and read/write roles. The supplied test installation is\nNextcloud 35.0.2 with `AppAPI` 35.0.0; no `ExApp` or deployment daemon was registered initially.\n\n## Alternatives\nPublishing the existing server exposes unowned recipes and operator-token health APIs.\nPer-user directories cannot represent shared households. A shared instance-wide directory has\nno household boundary. A new general repository framework or authentication service is unnecessary.\n\n## Decision\nAdd an opt-in `AppAPI` server mode with authenticated lifecycle, registered top menu/assets,\nallowlisted proxy routes and server-side household membership. Keep the local mode intact.\nAuthenticate the signed `AppAPI` user/secret pair with constant-time comparison; never trust a\nbrowser-supplied user ID or expose the `ExApp` secret/operator credentials to the browser.\nStore membership separately from domain data. Each household has dedicated existing recipe,\nprofile and planning stores, preserving revision and nutrition provenance rules. Check current\nroles before forwarding any request; readers cannot write even when a domain route lacks its\nown local role check. Invitations are addressed, explicit and revocable.\n\nUse Vue 3, `@nextcloud/vue` 9.14.1 and a single Vite IIFE plus CSS for `AppAPI`'s actual script loader.\nUse `@nextcloud/router` to generate the embedded history base and PHP proxy URLs, and Nextcloud\naxios for session/CSRF behavior. No origin hardcoding, CSP relaxation or CORS workaround.\nSchema-validate responses at the `TypeScript` boundary. Keep decimal amounts as strings.\n\nAdditional Rust dependencies must serve this accepted boundary: base64 for `AppAPI` credentials,\nsubtle for constant-time comparison, reqwest for authenticated Nextcloud OCS registration, and\ntower util for dispatching already authorized requests into existing domain routers. No new crate\nwithin the workspace and no new storage adapter are introduced.\n\n## Consequences\nNextcloud identity supplies authentication; household membership supplies authorization.\nDomain data is not migrated from arbitrary local directories. Unknown data remains unknown.\nThe local test daemon is a development deployment, not evidence for `HaRP`/container packaging.\nF22/F18 stay In progress until their complete acceptance criteria have actual evidence.\n\n## Dependency and packaging evidence\nNextcloud Vue 9.14.1 depends on Vue Router 5.4; using Router 4 alongside it breaks router-aware\nnative buttons. The client uses one Router 5 instance. `AppAPI` 35 appends `.js`/`.css` to its\nregistered asset paths; registration therefore uses `js/mealshop` and `css/mealshop`.\nThe Vite IIFE defines Nextcloud Vue's `appName`, `appVersion` and production environment.\nThe FRP entrypoint is copied from the installed Nextcloud exapp-development reference with its\ncopyright and licence intact; `appinfo/info.xml` uses a local-only image registry, not a published\nrelease. TCP and `HaRP` Unix-socket transports use the same authenticated routes.\n\nRust's TLS chain introduces ISC (`ring`, `rustls-webpki`, `untrusted`) and the bundled trust-store\nCDLA-Permissive-2.0 (`webpki-roots`) licences. deny.toml grants only exact-version exceptions for\nthese packages, retaining the existing default policy. `RustSec` findings in quinn-proto and\nwebpki-roots were resolved with patched versions, without advisory ignores. A minimatch override\naddresses the npm advisory in the Nextcloud component dependency graph; no component downgrade.\n\nPrimary integration reference: [Nextcloud 35 `ExApp` overview](https://docs.nextcloud.com/server/stable/developer_manual/exapp_development/development_overview/ExAppOverview.html).\nThe installed `AppAPI` 35.0.0 PHP controller and asset services were also read and tested directly.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_adr_0012_nextcloud_shared_households_md {}

#[doc = "# Architecture decisions\n\nUse the next free four-digit number. Record context, alternatives, decision, consequences\nand status; supersede accepted decisions explicitly rather than silently rewriting history.\nA proposal is not customer acceptance of an unresolved choice.\n\n- [ADR 0001: stack and delivery](../page_docs_adr_0001_stack_and_delivery_md/index.html) — Accepted for bootstrap; future integrations planned.\n- [ADR 0002: quantities and nutrition](../page_docs_adr_0002_quantities_and_nutrition_md/index.html) — Proposed implementation; customer unit preferences accepted.\n- [ADR 0003: persistence and data boundaries](../page_docs_adr_0003_persistence_and_data_boundaries_md/index.html) — Customer storage targets accepted; schema and adapters proposed.\n- [ADR 0004: licensing hosting and sharing](../page_docs_adr_0004_licensing_hosting_and_sharing_md/index.html) — AGPL and business goals accepted; admission/commerce/federation proposed.\n- [ADR 0005: privacy and learning](../page_docs_adr_0005_privacy_and_learning_md/index.html) — Proposed safeguards for customer-requested learning.\n- [ADR 0006: durable process schedules](../page_docs_adr_0006_durable_process_schedules_md/index.html) — Proposed shared process model.\n- [ADR 0007: localization and web clients](../page_docs_adr_0007_localization_and_web_clients_md/index.html) — Customer direction accepted; product integration proposed.\n- [ADR 0008: curated ingredient sources](../page_docs_adr_0008_curated_ingredient_sources_md/index.html) — Customer scope accepted; import/model details proposed.\n\n- [ADR 0009: local recipe revisions](../page_docs_adr_0009_local_recipe_revisions_md/index.html) — Local authoring slice; review pending.\n\n- [ADR 0010: protected local household access](../page_docs_adr_0010_local_household_access_md/index.html) — Proposed local F09 slice.\n\n- [ADR 0011: family meal planning](../page_docs_adr_0011_family_meal_planning_md/index.html) — Local protected preview/apply and consented attendance import.\n\n- [0012: native Nextcloud client and shared household boundary](../page_docs_adr_0012_nextcloud_shared_households_md/index.html)\n"]
