#[doc = "# Development and delivery\n\nStart with AGENTS.md and the affected spec. Use stable Rust; Cargo.toml declares the tested\nMSRV. Cargo.lock is committed; CI uses --locked. Unit preferences are presentation settings,\nnot a reason to erase source quantities. Keep core calculations independent of HTTP/storage.\n\n## Local gate\n```sh\npython3 scripts/generate_docs.py --check\npython3 scripts/import_catalogue.py --check\npython3 scripts/verify_catalogue.py\nnode --check src/web/demo.mjs\nnode --test tests/i18n.test.mjs\ncargo fmt --all --check\ncargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings\ncargo nextest run --workspace --locked\ncargo build --release --locked\ncargo deny check licenses\ncargo audit\nRUSTDOCFLAGS=\"-D warnings\" cargo doc --workspace --no-deps --locked\ncargo msrv verify -- cargo check --all-targets --locked\n```\nInstall cargo-nextest, cargo-deny, cargo-audit and cargo-msrv if absent. CI additionally\nuses cargo-llvm-cov and cargo-sbom. Tests may unwrap assertion setup; production may not.\n\n## Workflows\n- CI: fmt/clippy, nextest/coverage, MSRV, debug/release artifacts, SPDX SBOM and size metrics.\n- Security audit: `RustSec` advisory checks and dependency licence policy; runs daily as well.\n- Docs: strict rustdoc with requirements/specs/ADRs embedded, uploaded and deployed to Pages.\n- Renovate: daily self-hosted Renovate using `RENOVATE_TOKEN` (existing repository secret).\n\nAll workflows support `workflow_dispatch`. GitHub Pages must use the GitHub Actions build\nsource. Workflow permissions are scoped to the jobs that publish or create audit issues.\nNo upstream advisory ignores, ESP targets or machine-specific paths are retained.\nTo repeat a run: `gh workflow run <file>.yml --ref main --repo Th3Link/mealshop`.\nInspect `gh run list` and failed job logs; a dispatched workflow is not evidence of success.\n\n## Documentation\n`src/docs.rs` embeds the Markdown sources as rustdoc modules. Run `cargo doc --no-deps` and\nopen `target/doc/mealshop_server/docs/index.html`. The Docs workflow adds a root landing\npage. After adding a spec or ADR, update its index; docs generation discovers Markdown files.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_guides_development_md {}

#[doc = "# Protected local household profiles\n\nEnable the F09 slice by providing `MEALSHOP_HOUSEHOLD_TOKEN` (32–200 random ASCII letters,\ndigits, underscores or hyphens). Generate a strong operator secret with your normal secret\nmanager; do not commit it or put it in URLs. Without this variable household routes are absent.\nThe executable remains loopback-only. This is one household with one operator credential,\nnot a hosted account system. An optional distinct `MEALSHOP_HOUSEHOLD_READ_TOKEN` grants profile export and dietary evaluation only; writes/removals require the operator token. Anyone holding that credential can read/export, correct\nand remove every profile in this household. Revocation/rotation means changing the configured\ntoken and restarting; no token is written into profile files or browser storage.\n\n`MEALSHOP_HOUSEHOLD_DIR` defaults to `var/household`. Use a dedicated local directory separate\nfrom recipe storage. Newly created Unix directories/files use 0700/0600; existing directory\npermissions stay intact. Profiles use a schema-1, revisioned `profiles.json` snapshot (at most\n100 members); no profile revision history is retained. Editing/deletion replaces the snapshot.\nThis removes members from subsequent API reads; operator backups and previously exported\ncopies are outside server deletion. Backups contain sensitive profile data and require the\nsame access restrictions as the live store.\n\nThe demo asks for the operator token in a password field; it stays in page memory only.\nLoad profiles before editing. Select an existing member or New member; allergy/intolerance\nnotes are separate from hard ingredient exclusions and soft dislikes. Save or remove submits\nthe full household snapshot with its expected revision. A stale edit returns a conflict; copy\nunsaved changes before reloading/reconciling. The review button evaluates current recipe\ningredient identities for every recorded member. No profile data or token is logged.\n\n## API contracts\nAll endpoints require `Authorization: Bearer <operator token>` and return `Cache-Control:\nno-store`. GET `/api/household/profiles` exports the complete schema/revision/member JSON.\nPUT the same path with `{\"expected_revision\":0,\"members\":[...]}` creates/updates/removes\nmembers by replacement. A member has `id` (lowercase identifier), `name`, `allergies` (text\nnotes), `allergen_ids` (structured hard groups), `intolerances` (separate review notes), `excluded_ingredients` and `disliked_ingredients` (canonical catalogue ID arrays).\nRemoving a member means omitting it from the replacement array; an empty array removes all.\nNo separate unauthenticated member lookup or profile route exists.\n\nPOST `/api/household/evaluate` accepts `member_ids` and `ingredient_ids`. Hard ingredient\nexclusions are unioned across every selected member and return `blocked`. Known English/German\nnames/aliases are resolved conservatively to matching catalogue identities for exclusion\nchecks; ambiguous names never establish safety. Dislikes are returned separately and cannot\nremove a hard exclusion. Unknown ingredients remain explicitly listed. Even with no exclusion,\nstatus is `review_required`, never safe: the catalogue lacks complete allergen mappings and\ncross-contamination data. Allergy/intolerance notes are retained for human review; this slice\ndoes not infer an allergen ontology from unreviewed text. No learning mechanism can mutate\nhard constraints. Group selection must reference existing household members.\n\n## Persistence and recovery\nWrites run on blocking workers, validate the whole replacement, sync a private `.pending`\nfile, rename it over the current snapshot, and sync the directory on Unix. Ambiguous I/O\nfailures disable further writes until operator recovery. Startup rejects malformed/unknown\nschemas, invalid IDs, oversized data and an interrupted pending write. Exclusive `.household.lock`\nprevents concurrent process writers. After a crash, stop all writers, back up the entire\nprotected directory and inspect the snapshot/pending file before removing a verified stale\nlock. Preserve only a validated current snapshot in the recovery directory; never blindly\nreplace a good snapshot with an incomplete pending file. Restart and verify authorized export.\nNo automatic Git history, cloud sync or external writers are supported. Linux local filesystems\nare the initial target; cross-platform crash durability has not been validated.\n\n## Structured allergen constraints\nGET `/api/household/allergens` returns the versioned reference registry to authorized readers.\nThe demo offers 14 English-base allergen groups with German labels. Selected `allergen_ids`\nare hard constraints. 106 positive food-identity classifications retain exact source food IDs,\nforms and an FSA source reference. These are reference identity classifications, not laboratory\nmeasurements of retail products. Milk-group constraints also match explicitly named lactose-free\nmilk; absence of lactose does not remove the milk identity. See the source selection documentation\nin data/README.md. Other allergy and intolerance notes remain review-required; exact legacy group\nIDs in those notes are also treated conservatively as hard constraints. No natural-language\ninterpretation or personalised threshold is inferred.\n\nResults include `allergen_matches` with member/ingredient/group/source, the classification version,\nhard ingredient matches and separate dislikes. Every unblocked result requires review because\nallergen coverage is positive-only and cross-contact is unmodelled. Hosted user management and\nadditional source coverage do not alter this rule. Read-only credentials cannot correct or\nremove members; attempts return 403. Unknown credentials return 401. Rotate either configured\ncredential by restarting. Existing schema-1 profiles load with empty new fields.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_guides_household_md {}

#[doc = "# Family meal planning\n\nEnable household access as described in household.md. The executable links recipes and protected\nplanning automatically when `MEALSHOP_HOUSEHOLD_TOKEN` is configured. Embedding applications use\n`family_router`; separate standalone household builders have no recipe store and planning returns\n`recipe_store_unavailable`. Operator and reader roles are the same as F09. No tokens or profile\nsnapshots are stored in browser storage. Planning responses are `no-store` and data is not logged.\n\n## Demo flow\nEnter the household token, then Load family and saved plans. Choose date/meal, attendees,\nrecipe revision, target servings and available active/elapsed/advance seconds. Use the candidate\nmulti-select (maximum 50) to request suggestions. A manual selection or suggested alternative\nmust be previewed before applying. Preview shows exclusions, selected members' current review\nnotes, duration estimates, scaled nutrition and signed changes in required shopping grams.\nBlocked candidates cannot be applied. Every unblocked meal still requires explicit review of\nunknown dietary coverage and batch-time uncertainty. Changing inputs invalidates the preview.\n\nAfter acknowledgement, Apply saves the meal slot and refreshes aggregate shopping requirements\nfor all saved slots. Load a saved meal to change attendance/recipe/servings/time. Removal has its\nown negative-quantity preview and operator-only apply. No shopping item is purchased or external\nlist modified. A profile/plan revision conflict requires reloading and a fresh preview; stale\nchoices are never silently applied. Profile/reference changes mark saved slots for renewed review.\n\n## Read-only calendar import\nThe file input accepts an attendance JSON snapshot up to 32 KiB. Example:\n```json\n{\"source\":\"Family calendar export\",\"event_id\":\"event-42\",\"date\":\"2026-11-09\",\"member_ids\":[\"member-id-from-household-export\"]}\n```\nConsent must be checked after import; merely choosing a file does not consent. Check Use calendar\nattendance to use its proposal; uncheck it and use the attendee selector to override. Source,\ndate and proposed IDs stay visible. Calendar date must match the meal date. Clear removes the\nsnapshot from the draft. Member IDs must already be mapped to this household; unmapped attendees\ncannot silently become missing dietary constraints. No network calendar connection, recurrence\nexpansion or external calendar write occurs. This adapter consumes read-only snapshot data;\nprovider integrations remain separate deployment work.\n\n## API\nGET `/api/household/mealplan` returns schema/revision, saved meals, current profile revision,\nslots requiring renewed review and derived `shopping_requirements`.\nPOST `/api/household/mealplan/preview` accepts:\n```json\n{\"date\":\"2026-11-09\",\"meal\":\"dinner\",\"recipe\":{\"id\":\"recipe-00000001\",\"revision\":1},\n \"attendees\":[\"member-id\"],\"calendar\":null,\"servings\":2,\n \"active_seconds\":1800,\"elapsed_seconds\":3600,\"advance_seconds\":0}\n```\n`attendees: null` uses `calendar.member_ids`; that calendar object additionally requires\n`consent: true`. Explicit attendees override it. Targets are 1–1000000 integer servings.\nActive/meal-day elapsed budgets are 0–86400 seconds; advance lead is 0–2592000 seconds.\nMeal keys are lowercase ASCII letters/underscores, 1–40 bytes. Dates are validated, including\nmonth lengths/leap years. Empty steps yield `preparation_time_unknown`; totals are sequential\nrecipe estimates and do not infer parallel work or batch-time scaling.\n\nPreview returns `plan`, `profile_revision`, `plan_revision`, resolved attendees/source/override,\nF09 review evidence, `feasible`, conflict codes, active/elapsed/advance timing, rationally scaled\nnutrition and signed `shopping_delta`. No persistence occurs.\n\nPOST `/api/household/mealplan/suggestions` takes `{\"template\":{...plan...},\"candidates\":[\n{\"id\":\"recipe-00000001\",\"revision\":1}]}` (1–50 candidates). Every rejected candidate includes\nconflicts/errors. No match returns `no_feasible_meal`; preferences cannot relax exclusions.\n\nPOST `/api/household/mealplan/apply` takes `{\"plan\":{...},\"expected_revision\":0,\n\"profile_revision\":1,\"reviewed\":true}`. Use the revisions from the preview. Matching date/meal\nreplaces one slot; recipe revisions stay pinned. A conflict returns 409; hard/time conflicts or\nmissing review return 422. Readers cannot apply (403). Acknowledgement means human review, not\nan allergen-safety certificate. The server recomputes constraints; client `feasible` fields are\nnot accepted. GET subsequently derives shopping quantities from the saved pinned revisions.\n\nPOST `/api/household/mealplan/remove-preview` with the meal settings previews removing that\nslot. Apply with `remove: true`, current revisions and review acknowledgement removes it.\nThe removal preview carries the correct negative quantities, including after constraints change.\n\n## Storage/recovery\n`mealplan.json` is a schema-1 snapshot in the protected household directory, at most 100 slots\nand 1 MiB. The existing household process lock covers it. Writes use private `.mealplan.pending`,\nfile sync, rename and Unix directory sync. Ambiguous I/O failure disables further plan writes;\nprofiles remain independently accessible. Startup rejects malformed, oversized, duplicate-slot\nor interrupted plans instead of silently creating an empty planner. Export JSON and backups can\ncontain attendance and calendar IDs; protect them like profiles. Removing profiles does not\nrewrite existing plans; remove affected slots explicitly if their historical attendance must\nalso be removed. Previously exported copies/backups are outside server revocation.\n\nFor recovery, stop all writers and back up both recipe and household directories consistently.\nInspect any pending file and snapshot before removing a verified stale household lock. Work on\na recovery copy, preserve all recipe revisions referenced by plans, restore a validated complete\nsnapshot, then verify protected plan export, nutrition and shopping quantities. Do not directly\nedit files while the application is running. Network filesystems and cross-platform crash\nbehaviour are not validated; Linux local filesystems are the initial target.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_guides_meal_planning_md {}

#[doc = "# Native Nextcloud client\n\nMealshop has an opt-in `AppAPI` transport and Vue 3 client for shared households. The existing\nloopback-only server/demo remains available. This guide describes the current test deployment;\nit does not claim a published app-store release or a verified production `HaRP` installation.\n\n## Verified target and client structure\n\nTarget installation: Nextcloud 35.0.2, `AppAPI` 35.0.0. Client: Vue 3, Composition API,\nstrict `TypeScript`, `@nextcloud/vue` 9.14.1, Vue Router 5.4 and Vite 7. The `AppAPI` loader\nreceives one IIFE (`js/mealshop.js`) and stylesheet (`css/mealshop.css`). It adds extensions to\nregistered paths, so registrations use `js/mealshop` and `css/mealshop`.\n\n`NcContent`/`NcAppNavigation`/`NcAppContent` use the actual Nextcloud header, theme, navigation\nand skip actions. Application CSS is scoped to `.mealshop`; no reset, web font or replacement\nshell is installed. The router base and API proxy use `@nextcloud/router`, preserving webroot\nand `index.php` behavior. No origin is embedded in product code.\n\n## Application workflows\n\nCreate a household or accept an invitation addressed to your exact Nextcloud user ID.\nOwners manage invitations and current roles; editors modify recipes, dietary profiles and\nplans; readers can inspect, export and review without applying changes. Invitations explicitly\nexplain access to allergy information. Dietary profiles are people eating, distinct from the\nNextcloud accounts granted access. Use **Refresh access** after another person accepts; this\nupdates access without overwriting unsaved dietary edits. Non-owners can leave from the\nhousehold list; ownership must first be transferred to another accepted member.\n\nRecipes support creation, immutable revisions, original-language content and exact serving\npreviews. Ingredient quantities accept g, kg, ml, l, tsp, tbsp and pieces. Non-mass inputs need\nan explicitly selected exact-record reference portion or declared density/piece/spoon evidence.\nThe original amount, unit and evidence persist; no nearby food record supplies missing data.\nThe per-browser recipe display preference requests g/kg presentation from Rust. Kilograms may\nneed nine fractional digits to represent normalized micrograms exactly; this changes neither\nstored input precision nor ingredient quantities. Repeated edits use expected revisions.\nConflicts retain the draft and require an explicit reload/review rather than silent overwrite.\n\nProfiles separate structured hard allergen exclusions, free-text allergy/intolerance notes,\nexcluded ingredient IDs and soft dislikes. Export uses the current saved profile revision;\nremoval is confirmed and saved as a replacement snapshot. Unknown coverage stays unknown.\n\nPlanning uses pinned recipe revisions and titles, actual attendees, active/elapsed/advance\nbudgets, explicit calendar-snapshot consent and override, suggestions, review, shopping mass\ndeltas, apply and removal. Suggestions explain rejected constraints. Hard conflicts cannot be\nacknowledged away. Reference batch times and uncertainty are visible before apply. No live\ncalendar provider or purchase workflow is implied by the snapshot and quantity preview.\n\nThe catalogue searches English/German names and aliases, retains exact nutrient strings,\ndistinguishes unknown values from measured zero, and exposes source/version/licence/uncertainty.\nSource educational content stays original and marked as such when no reviewed translation exists.\nUI language is a browser preference; invalid quantity text is never silently reinterpreted when\nswitching languages. No token, recipe or health profile is stored in browser storage.\n\n## Trust boundaries\n\nThe transport validates `EX-APP-ID` and the base64 user/secret pair from `AUTHORIZATION-APP-API`\nwith constant-time secret comparison. Anonymous heartbeat is the only public infrastructure\nendpoint. Empty service users cannot access household APIs. Current membership is checked for\nevery request before forwarding into a dedicated existing recipe/profile/plan store. Browser\nBearer credentials and cookies never select the household authority; the internal operator token\nis supplied server-side only. All domain responses containing household data are `no-store`.\n\n`AppAPI` 35's PHP proxy is explicitly `NoCSRFRequired`. Mealshop therefore requires\n`X-Mealshop-Request: 1` on browser writes, in addition to authenticated identity and authorization,\nand requires JSON content type for membership bodies. Cross-origin simple forms cannot set the\ncustom header; no CORS allowance or OPTIONS route is added. Nextcloud axios retains native\nsession/request-token behavior. The app does not relax the host CSP or log request credentials,\nsource recipe bodies or dietary profiles.\n\nMembership uses a private, bounded, atomically replaced registry with one owner per household,\naddressed invitations, an exclusive writer lock and fail-closed corrupt/pending-file handling.\nThe current principals are Nextcloud user IDs: revoke household access before deleting/reusing\nan account ID. Automated account-deletion reconciliation and identity-backend migrations are\nnot implemented by this frontend slice. Do not treat this test deployment as production admission.\n\n## Local development deployment\n\nBuild with supported Node 24 (minimum 24.15), npm 12.2.2 and Rust:\n\n```sh\ncd clients/nextcloud\nnpm ci --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund\nnpm run build\ncd ../..\ncargo build --locked\n```\n\nProvide a private JSON environment file (mode `0600`) with string values for `APP_SECRET`\n(32–200 ASCII letters/digits/underscore/hyphen), `APP_HOST`, `APP_PORT`, `NEXTCLOUD_URL`,\n`APP_PERSISTENT_STORAGE` and absolute `MEALSHOP_ASSET_DIR`. Never commit it. Register a\nmanual-install daemon pointing at an address reachable by Nextcloud and start:\n\n```sh\npython3 scripts/run_nextcloud.py /absolute/path/to/private-runtime.json\n```\n\nRegister `mealshop` with that daemon, the same private secret/port, and the `USER` routes in\n`appinfo/info.xml`. The authenticated enable hook idempotently registers menu and assets.\nNo `/init` work is required: 404 means initialization is unnecessary. Startup restores the\nactual enabled state from Nextcloud; disabling denies subsequent domain requests. SIGINT/SIGTERM\nrelease storage locks cleanly. Do not remove a live writer's lock; follow the existing recipe\nand household recovery guides after an interrupted write. Restart only one writer per directory.\n\nOn the supplied test machine, the private runtime files are under ignored `var/nextcloud/`;\nthe active app data is `/data/mealshop-nextcloud/mealshop`, separate from Nextcloud's `html`.\nA pre-migration copy of the initial synthetic app data remains under ignored\n`var/nextcloud/data`. The Nextcloud reset script has not been used.\n\n## Container artifact and verified test deployment\n\n```sh\ndocker build -t mealshop.local/mealshop:0.1.0 .\npython3 scripts/test_exapp_container.py\n```\n\nThe image pins base digests, builds Rust with 1.85 and the Vue IIFE with Node 24/npm 12.2.2,\nand includes the manifest, assets, CA store and FRP entrypoint. Runtime uses a supported Alpine\n3.24 release. The isolated smoke test exercises both TCP and Unix socket, lifecycle registration,\nassets, authorization, CSRF and private storage as UID 1000 with a read-only root filesystem.\nIts OCS mock is a transport fixture, not evidence of a real `HaRP` tunnel.\n\nThe copied Nextcloud reference entrypoint retains its copyright/licence. Local adaptations set\n`umask 077` and put its FRP configuration in `/tmp/frpc.toml` for rootless/read-only operation.\n`HP_SHARED_KEY` selects `/tmp/exapp.sock`; otherwise `APP_HOST`/`APP_PORT` select TCP.\nThe manifest uses a local-only image registry; nothing has been published to it.\n\n`deploy/nextcloud/compose.yaml` can attach a separately managed test container to the existing\nNextcloud network, with the same secret and data, no added capabilities and an internal bridge\nport binding. It requires private environment/settings files for the image, data owner UID/GID,\nport, data path and external network. It does not modify the original Nextcloud Compose file.\n\nThe customer explicitly approved adding the internal service hostname `nextcloud` to the test\ninstance's `trusted_domains` on 2026-11-09. It was added at index 3, preserving existing entries.\nThe manual process was gracefully stopped before starting `mealshop-exapp-test-mealshop-1`\nwith the same private secret, port and `/data/mealshop-nextcloud/mealshop` directory. The original\nNextcloud Compose configuration was not modified. The image runs as the existing data owner,\nwith a read-only root filesystem, no additional capabilities and `no-new-privileges`.\n\nManage this deployment from the repository root using the private configuration:\n\n```sh\ndocker compose --env-file var/nextcloud/compose.env -f deploy/nextcloud/compose.yaml ps\ndocker compose --env-file var/nextcloud/compose.env -f deploy/nextcloud/compose.yaml restart mealshop\n```\n\nThe manual startup helper remains available for development, but must not run concurrently\nagainst the same directory. Back up the Nextcloud registry/config and app data together before\nchanging identities or resetting the instance. See F22 validation for restart and browser evidence.\n\n## Repeatable checks\n\n```sh\ncd clients/nextcloud\nnpm run format:check\nnpm test\nnpm run build\nnpm audit --audit-level=moderate\nMEALSHOP_TEST_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080 \\\n  MEALSHOP_TEST_USER=your-test-owner MEALSHOP_TEST_PASSWORD=your-test-password \\\n  MEALSHOP_READER_USER=your-test-reader MEALSHOP_READER_PASSWORD=your-reader-password \\\n  npm run test:e2e\n```\n\nThe browser suite creates fictional household/recipe/profile data. Use an explicitly disposable\ninstance and two prepared accounts; do not point it at personal household data. It verifies the\nactual host, read/write boundaries, revocation, conflicts, exact quantities, hard constraints,\nsource portions, deep-link refresh, responsive layout, native light/dark, keyboard and axe checks.\nScreenshots/traces remain local in ignored test-result directories. Formal accessibility\ncertification, a live calendar connector, standalone shell and Flutter are separate work.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_guides_nextcloud_md {}

#[doc = "# F02: ingredient catalogue and nutrition\n\nStatus: accepted through merged PR #4. This is a disposable test demo with\n313 ingredients and 17 nutrient/component definitions, not a production family planner.\n\n## Try the browser demo\n```sh\ncargo run --locked\n```\nOpen [the local workbench](http://127.0.0.1:3000/). The initial input is 100 g oats and 25 g\nchia for two servings. Choose **Calculate nutrition**: energy is 510.50 kcal for the recipe,\n255.25 kcal per serving; protein is 21.03 g for the recipe and 10.51 g per serving.\n\nChange chia to psyllium husks. Complete totals become unknown: the known energy subtotal\nremains 389.00 kcal for the recipe and 194.50 kcal per serving, explicitly labelled partial.\nChange preparation to **Will be cooked / yield unknown** to see the retention/yield warning.\nNegative amounts produce a visible error and preserve the input. Search the notebook by\nname or alias (for example Hafer); expand composition to inspect source values per 100 g.\n\nThe browser uses same-origin API calls and no third-party assets/analytics. It stores no\nrecipe, health or household data, and sends none to external nutrition providers.\n\n## HTTP API\n- `GET /health`: unchanged process-health contract (`ok` plus a newline).\n- `GET /api/ingredients?q=hafer`: catalogue version, name/alias-matched ingredient records\n  and nutrient definitions (ID, label, unit, group). Search is case-insensitive.\n- `GET /api/ingredients/oats`: the complete record including aliases, edible form, basis,\n  source/version/licence, uncertainty, nutrient readings and education. Missing IDs return 404.\n- `POST /api/nutrition/calculate`: stateless source-based aggregation. Invalid amounts,\n  unsupported fields, unknown ingredient IDs or infeasible bounds return a structured JSON\n  error. No mutation endpoint is provided.\n\n```sh\ncurl --fail-with-body http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/nutrition/calculate \\\n  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\\n  --data '{\"lines\":[{\"ingredient_id\":\"oats\",\"grams\":\"100\"},{\"ingredient_id\":\"chia\",\"grams\":\"25\"}],\"servings\":2,\"preparation\":\"as_recorded\"}'\n```\n\nEach nutrient result names its unit and coverage (`complete`, `partial`, `unknown`). `total`\nand `per_serving` are decimal strings only when every contributing ingredient has a known\nvalue; otherwise they are null. `known_total`/`known_per_serving` are labelled subtotals,\nnot complete values. For an entirely unknown nutrient, those known subtotals are zero\nbecause no known contribution exists; status/null totals preserve that distinction.\nMissing ingredient IDs are deduplicated. Repeated recipe lines still contribute separately.\nThe result retains original gram strings, source records, form/basis, catalogue version,\nsource uncertainty, educational conditions/limitations and preparation context.\n\n## Quantity and calculation contract\nAll input masses are grams of the record's edible form; the supported basis is\n`per_100g_edible_portion`. No volume, piece, yield or raw/cooked conversion is inferred.\nInput source values and masses are decimal strings in [0, 1000000000], with at most six\nfractional digits and no sign/exponent/whitespace; recipe masses must be strictly positive.\nServings are integer counts in [1, 1000000]; requests contain 1–100 lines. These are\nbounded-arithmetic/input-validation limits for this implementation, not subscription limits.\n\nAmounts retain their original spelling and an integer scaled by 1000000. For each nutrient,\nthe numerator sums `source_atoms * gram_atoms` and the denominator is `100 * 1000000²`.\nThe per-serving denominator additionally multiplies by serving count. Checked integer\narithmetic preserves the entire fraction until final formatting, with two decimal places\nand half-away-from-zero rounding (all valid values are nonnegative). At the bounds, the\nscaled sum is at most 10³² and the rounding numerator at most 10³⁴, within a 128-bit unsigned\ninteger. Portion values are never calculated from rounded recipe totals. This is arithmetic\nprecision, not a claim that the source food data are accurate to two decimals.\n\nThe registry distinguishes kcal/kJ, g, mg and µg. Carbohydrate specifically means the\nby-difference value including fibre; folate is total folate, not dietary folate equivalents.\nOther definitions require separate IDs before addition. Energy is retained from the source,\nnot recomputed from macros. Micronutrient coverage is a selected starter subset, not exhaustive.\n\n## Use an operator-provided catalogue\n```sh\nMEALSHOP_CATALOGUE_PATH=/absolute/path/catalogue.json cargo run --locked\n```\nThis file replaces the entire bundled catalogue at startup. The schema matches\n[data/catalogue.json](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/blob/main/data/catalogue.json). Schema 1 requires a nonempty version;\ningredient IDs are unique ASCII identifiers. Records need aliases (possibly empty), form,\nbasis, full source fields, uncertainty, a nutrient map and education (possibly empty).\nNutrient readings contain `amount` (decimal string or null) and `uncertainty`. Omitted nutrient\nkeys are also unknown. Only registry nutrient IDs are accepted; units are fixed by that registry.\nSources and evidence links use HTTPS. Education requires component, title, summary, evidence\nreference, conditions and limitations, independent of measured nutrient amounts.\n\nThe immutable catalogue is public operator-provided reference data. It is not a place for\nprivate household/health data. Catalogue writes/authentication, persistent ingredient editing,\nuser unit preferences, cooking retention models and the Flutter shell remain separate\nfeatures (F03/F09/F18). The [local recipe authoring slice](../page_docs_guides_recipes_md/index.html) now saves recipes\nand pins catalogue subsets independently of this public reference dataset.\n\n## Limits and provenance\nJSON recipe bodies are limited to 128 KiB; catalogue input to 1 MiB and 1000 records.\nThe server rejects malformed/oversized/unsupported catalogues and exits on missing files.\nAn empty catalogue is valid and the browser explains why calculation is unavailable.\nSource dates, sample counts and educational references are snapshots, not live provider sync.\n\nSee [starter data provenance](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/blob/main/data/README.md) and\n[F02 acceptance criteria](../page_specs_0002_ingredients_nutrition_specification_md/index.html).\n\n## Demo versus product UI\nThe customer classified this surface as a pragmatic demo. Keep design effort minimal.\nNextcloud/standalone product shells are F22 (Vue 3); Flutter is separate in F18.\nF21 starts with German/English demo messages, starter names and exact decimal presentation.\nSource uncertainty and educational text remain explicitly original-language until reviewed\ntranslations are available. UI language does not change nutrient IDs, units, source facts or\ncanonical API decimals. German entry uses a comma; English uses a dot, without grouping.\n\n## English base and localized demo\nCanonical IDs/names remain English; German ingredient names/aliases/forms use `translations.de`.\nThe language selector changes keyed interface messages and display, not nutrient values or stored\nAPI decimals. Enter e.g. `100,5` grams in German or `100.5` in English, without grouping separators.\nValid inputs/results survive language changes exactly. Previously invalid inputs require an\nexplicit edit before they can be reinterpreted in a new locale. Unsupported languages fall back to English.\nSearch covers both languages and common aliases/plurals (e.g. Weizen 405, Hafermilch, Eier).\n\nThe 313-item kitchen catalogue is generated offline from reviewed BLS/USDA selections; see\n[data provenance and rebuild instructions](https://github.com/Th3Link/mealshop/blob/main/data/README.md). Available carbohydrate and\nby-difference carbohydrate are separate definitions; food folate is separate from total folate.\nMixed-source recipes may show partial values for these definitions, with an explanatory warning.\nError responses add stable `code` alongside the English `error` text; calculation results add\n`warning_codes` parallel to the retained English `warnings`. The demo localizes these codes.\n\n## Typed quantity conversion (F03 initial slice)\n`POST /api/quantities/convert` accepts `{\"quantity\":{\"amount\":\"1.250000\",\"unit\":\"kg\"},\"target\":\"g\"}` and returns the original input, its dimension and an exact converted quantity. Supported unit identifiers are `g`, `kg`, `ml`, `l`, `tsp`, `tbsp` and `piece`. Cross-dimension and spoon conversions require ingredient data or a named standard and return stable error codes. Conversion rejects values outside the six-decimal precision bound rather than rounding. This endpoint is not yet connected to recipes or user preferences.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_guides_nutrition_md {}

#[doc = "# Local recipe authoring\n\nThe executable stores locally authored recipes in `var/recipes` (ignored by Git). Override\nwith `MEALSHOP_RECIPE_DIR=/absolute/dedicated/path`; an empty value is an error. The directory\nis created on first run. Only loopback listen addresses are currently supported. There is no\nauthentication: this is a trusted local, single-household authoring slice, not hosted access.\nStop the server before operating on the storage files. Use a local filesystem supporting\nhard links/fsync; Linux is the initial validated target. Existing directory permissions are\nnot modified; newly created Unix directories/files use 0700/0600.\n\n## Demo\nUse the existing demo at `http://127.0.0.1:3000/`. Enter a title, author attribution, recipe\ntext language (English/German), catalogue ingredients and gram amounts, serving count and\noptional steps with active/passive seconds. German amounts use commas; API amounts use exact\ndot-decimal strings. Save creates a new recipe; saving a loaded recipe creates a revision.\nThe saved list and load button retrieve persisted recipes and their saved serving estimates.\nLoad/new actions explicitly replace inputs. Failed saves keep all inputs. On a revision\nconflict, copy your edits before loading the latest revision and reconciling them manually.\n\nChanging the authoring serving count divides the same batch. The separate scaling preview uses the saved original revision and rounds each ingredient half up to 0.000001 g. Nutrition uses the exact original serving ratio before display rounding. A\nsaved recipe uses its own source snapshot, even after the catalogue changes. Saving a new\nrevision uses the current catalogue and recalculates nutrition. Missing current ingredient\nIDs cannot be silently substituted: restore the catalogue or explicitly replace them.\nUnknown nutrient values remain unknown. Authors are self-reported, not authenticated.\nThis remains a pragmatic demo; product clients and process timers are separate features.\n\n## API\n```sh\ncurl --fail-with-body http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/recipes \\\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{\n    \"title\":\"Porridge\", \"language\":\"en\", \"author\":\"Local cook\",\n    \"servings\":2, \"preparation\":\"cooking_unmodelled\",\n    \"lines\":[{\"ingredient_id\":\"oats\",\"grams\":\"100.500000\"},\n             {\"ingredient_id\":\"chia\",\"grams\":\"25\"}],\n    \"steps\":[{\"instruction\":\"Mix and rest.\",\"active_seconds\":60,\"passive_seconds\":300}]\n  }'\n```\nPOST `/api/recipes` returns HTTP 201 with `id`, `revision`, `recipe` and `nutrition`.\nGET `/api/recipes` lists latest summaries. GET `/api/recipes/recipe-00000001` retrieves the\nlatest recipe and estimate; append `?revision=1` for a historical revision. PUT that same\npath with `{\"expected_revision\":1,\"recipe\":{...complete recipe fields...}}` creates revision 2.\nConcurrent stale writes return HTTP 409 / `revision_conflict`. No PATCH or DELETE is offered.\nOther stable codes include `invalid_recipe`, `recipe_not_found`, `invalid_revision`,\n`recipe_storage_full` and `recipe_storage_failed`. Recipe responses are marked `no-store`.\nRecipe IDs and revisions are local to this store, not globally federated identifiers.\n\nBounds: 128 KiB body; 1–100 ingredient lines; positive amounts up to 1000000000 g with six\nfractional digits; 1–1000000 integer servings; title/author 1–200 UTF-8 bytes; language en/de;\n0–100 steps with 1–4000 byte instructions and 0–604800 seconds per active/passive duration.\nThe local store holds at most 1000 immutable revisions, each at most 2 MiB. It does not delete\nold revisions to make room. Capacity expansion/migration belongs to F18.\n\n## Backup and recovery\nStop all processes using the directory; copy the entire directory (including every revision)\nto a separate backup location. Never edit files while a server is running: reads use an\nin-memory index and external writers are unsupported. Restore into an empty dedicated directory,\nthen start the server and verify list/detail/portion results. Full adapter migration is not\nimplemented. Do not delete individual revisions: each recipe history must start at 1 and be\nconsecutive. Preserve the original backup when repairing any damaged copy.\n\nNormal graceful shutdown removes `.mealshop.lock`. After a crash it remains. Check that no\nprocess is using the directory before removing a stale lock; PID text is advisory, not proof\nthat no writer exists. Starting with a live/stale lock fails closed.\n\nA `.pending` file means publication may have been interrupted. Stop writers, back up the\nentire directory and inspect `.pending` plus its recipe ID/revision target. If the target\nexists and is byte-identical, it was published; retain the target and remove `.pending` from\nthe recovery copy. If no target exists, preserve `.pending` outside the live directory before\nremoving it there (the save was not published). If they differ, restore a known-good complete\nbackup or reconcile offline; never replace the target blindly. Remove only a verified stale\nlock and restart against the recovery copy. Startup validates schema, source snapshots,\nquantities, filenames and consecutive revision histories; corruption never becomes an empty\nstore. A storage error returns 503 and disables later writes until restart/recovery. After a\nnetwork timeout the save may have committed: refresh the list/history before retrying, since\nPOST retry idempotency is not implemented. Unacknowledged valid published revisions can be\nvisible after recovery, so inspect the latest revision before resubmitting edits.\n\n## Embedding\n`router()` / `router_from_catalogue_json()` remain stateless catalogue/nutrition applications.\n`router_with_recipe_directory(json, directory)` explicitly enables durable recipes and holds\nits exclusive lock until every cloned router/request state is dropped. The caller must enforce\nthe trusted local access boundary; the library does not provide authentication or listener policy.\n\nGET `/api/recipes/{id}/scale?servings=3&revision=1` previews a saved revision at another\nserving count without writing it. Omit revision for the latest. The response includes both\nserving counts, scaled gram strings and nutrition; unsupported or zero rounded amounts fail.\n\n## Original quantity and conversion evidence\nA recipe line can supply `quantity: {\"amount\":\"0.125\",\"unit\":\"kg\"}` instead of grams.\nThe server normalizes to grams and stores both. If both are supplied they must agree exactly.\nLegacy gram-only lines remain valid. For ml/l/piece/tsp/tbsp input supply `conversion` with\n`ingredient_id`, exact catalogue `form`, `source_url` (HTTPS), `version`, `uncertainty`,\n`grams_per_base_unit` (g/ml or g/piece), and optional `spoon_standard` and\n`millilitres_per_spoon`. Spoon input requires both optional fields; name the selected spoon\nand regional standard. These are user-declared evidence, not independently verified sources.\nCross-dimension arithmetic rejects unsupported precision; it never assumes water density.\nThe immutable revision retains this context. Scaling previews normalize from saved grams,\nleaving original inputs/evidence in the source revision. Local demo g/kg preference affects\nmass display only; other users' saved recipes are not rewritten.\n\n## Exact recipe display units\n\nRecipe detail and serving-preview GET requests accept optional `display_unit=g|kg`. The additive\n`display_lines` response contains server-formatted mass amounts and units. Kilograms retain up\nto nine fractional digits to represent normalized micrograms without rounding. This presentation\ndoes not change the stored source quantity, six-place input bound or nutrient calculation.\n"]
pub mod page_docs_guides_recipes_md {}

#[doc = "# Bootstrap provenance\n\nRetrieved 2026-11-07:\n- [curvyo/curvyo](https://github.com/curvyo/curvyo/tree/9ca3ebba86e511cd24559424f13fa2183caeea57):\n  AGENTS.md/CLAUDE.md conventions, specs/README.md lifecycle, docs/adr format and AGPL text.\n  Mealshop rewrites project-specific guidance; no Curvyo product modules or agent setup copied.\n- [Th3Link/homeautomation](https://github.com/Th3Link/homeautomation/tree/4c65a45d2046d795aaef5b79e3c311ae1f0a1847):\n  CI quality gate, nextest/coverage, cargo-msrv, debug/release build artifacts, cargo-sbom,\n  cargo-deny/audit, self-hosted Renovate and rustdoc/Pages publication patterns.\n  ESP/QEMU/firmware jobs do not apply. Metrics measure host server binary bytes, not ESP memory.\n  No upstream advisory exceptions or hardware-specific Renovate exclusions are carried over.\n\nThe initial customer request authorizes bootstrap and direct main publication. Future feature\nspecs remain Draft; they are not completed just because their documentation exists.\n"]
